new oversight measures are now in effect  for Greek life organizations at Penn  State University they were implemented  in response to the death of Timothy  Piazza earlier this year prosecutors  alleged the sophomore was hazed into  drinking excessive amounts of alcohol at  a party he has his parents push Penn  State to make changes and they are here  this morning with their reaction in an  interview you'll see only on CBS but  first here's a look at the tragedy that  led to the new measures preliminary  hearings are underway for members of the  now-closed Beta Theta Pi frat house 18  of them are facing charges in connection  to the death of sophomore Timothy Piazza  Piazza died in February following an  initiation ceremony that involved heavy  drinking he fell down a flight of stairs  and hit his head repeatedly often in  view of his fraternity brothers no one  called for help  until the following morning during  hearings in July the prosecution  presented apparently incriminating text  messages sent from some of the frat  brothers I don't want to go to jail for  this wrote one member the boys  themselves knew what they did the text  admit it right after before he even  passes away they say it's because of a  hazing it's because of the drinking we  caused this we should have called 9-1-1  but lawyers for the 16 defendants who  appeared at the preliminary hearing  claimed the evidence doesn't warrant a  trial some argue the brothers didn't  force Piazza to drink and can't be held  responsible for knowing the difference  between intoxication and serious injury  two of the brothers waived their right  to a preliminary hearing  now with the fall semester underway Penn  State President Eric Barron is  implementing more than a dozen reforms  to Greek life at the school the  university will take responsibility for  oversight and discipline of fraternities  and sororities social restrictions  addressing alcohol use will remain in  place as well as a no tolerance policy  for hazing Timothy Piazza's parents Jim  and Evelyn Piazza are with us now along  with their attorney Tom Klein good  morning Maureen thank you for being here  thank you  we've got the school year starting up  again it's been six months since your  son's death how are you doing it's hard  especially having to bring our son back  to school this past weekend this is your  Penn State it's your older son yeah yeah  yes so we were normally we make a couple  day trip out of it we were in and out  the same day we just couldn't couldn't  stick around it was it was hard knowing  that we should have been bringing two  kids back yeah Jim we now know that Penn  Penn State has announced some of these  changes which you have been pushing for  your family has been pushing for are  they enough well you know I think  they're a good start we've been in  contact with President Barron for the  past six months about proposals that  they need to implement and many of the  proposals that they've put forth or our  suggestions but there's still more that  they need to do they really haven't  implemented a lot of them yet and at the  end of the day they need to implement  and more importantly enforce but we're  happy to see that they've taken  responsibility and said that their  oversight has been severely lacking for  Greek life and we're happy that they're  taking responsibility for disciplinary  actions but they have the same people  involved and those people were  ineffective so I'm concerned about that  one of the defense attorneys argued in a  hearing recently that nobody forced Tim  your son to drink instead expecting  young men to be able to distinguish  between extreme intoxication and a head  injury quote he says quote places an  impossible burden on them what do you  say to those who argue that listen this  was just a tragic accident no they said  right away somebody texted that he fell  here first they knew he hit his head he  was unconscious he was having seizures  they knew that he was in a bad way and  who hasn't been involved with high  school sports and concussions you know  you know this stuff I just think it's an  absurd statement I mean he wanted to be  part of this club you know if they fed  significant amounts of alcohol into him  your judgment immediately starts to to  go sideways and they continued to force  alcohol on him and all the other pledges  you said force alcohol you don't  consider your son a reckless person  absolutely  tim was a good guy he was not a drinker  he how do you know he wasn't a drink  I've talked to his friends in his  roommates I asked them you know I was  concerned yeah and they said no he was  not he was not a big drinker  I think that these guys just put so much  alcohol and told these young men and and  you know and then didn't watch them let  them be on their own well that brings up  an interesting point a lot of parents  hope that there's an adult in the room  as kids are partying and there's a man  named Tim bream the fraternities adult  adviser he was in the house the night of  the party he declined our request for a  comment do you think he should be  punished yeah well let's get this  straight there were all adults  Tim bream we believe is culpable but  they were all adults they knew what they  were doing they committed crimes and  after they committed those crimes and  put our son in grave danger they all  went to bed they were all adults they  knew what they were doing and then they  tried to cover it up the next day when  they knew that it was bad the other  thing that's so difficult is there's a  tape there's it's on tape that's what so  surveillance video surveillance video  and I can't stop thinking about that you  agreed to watch it only if you watch it  with the university president and the  trustees and the trustees right and you  wanted to do that why well I only wanted  to do that so they would do it so they  can see the severity of what happened  and we want them to watch it we  personally do not want to watch it but  if they make as we will but they need to  watch it so that they can implement  these changes and again they need to  enforce without enforcement none of this  is really gonna do anything Tom we've  heard Penn State's presidents say that  the university will quote push for  statewide legislation to toughen  penalties for hazing is the law the  problem well the people are the problem  but law is a great deterrent and we're  working with senator Corman the majority  leader in Pennsylvania we're also  working with representative million in  the Congress and we're looking to change  the law we believe that stiffer  penalties will mean will mean deterrence  and that's a key but if if there's a  parent who's sending their son or  daughter to college what's the message  that you have about safety  go in with your eyes open talk to your  kids this could happen to anybody if it  happened to Tim it could happen to  anybody and if it doesn't feel right if  something makes you uncomfortable if you  feel pressured don't do it you're not  invincible you're so important to  somebody somewhere  I also encourage all the parents that if  they hear something that they should  also speak up and say something we've  heard a lot of stories about severe  hazing at Penn State now from a lot of  people some have written letters to the  president others just let it go you need  to speak up there's you know yeah you  could be home you're still grieving this  is still very recent but you were  speaking up because you want people to  know this needs to change and change now  it can't happen to anybody else it's  it's just too awful yep we have a big  stake in this this is part of Tim's  legacy we have to we have to make a  difference thank you thank you all thank  you thank you thanks Jim and Evelyn and  Tommy thank you all for joining us this  morning
 

